1
00:00:05,079 --> 00:00:11,839
It was a rimy morning, and very damp. I had seen the damp lying on the outside of my little window,

2
00:00:11,839 --> 00:00:16,920
as if some goblin had been crying there all night, and using the window for a pocket-handkerchief.

3
00:00:18,039 --> 00:00:25,720
Now, I saw the damp lying on the bare hedges and spare grass, like a coarser sort of spiders’ webs;

4
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:34,000
hanging itself from twig to twig and blade to blade. On every rail and gate, wet lay clammy,

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,160
and the marsh mist was so thick, that the wooden finger on the post directing

6
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:44,319
people to our village—a direction which they never accepted, for they never came there—was

7
00:00:44,319 --> 00:00:52,799
invisible to me until I was quite close under it. Then, as I looked up at it, while it dripped,

8
00:00:52,799 --> 00:00:58,400
it seemed to my oppressed conscience like a phantom devoting me to the Hulks.

9
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:03,400
The mist was heavier yet when I got out upon the marshes, so that instead of my running at

10
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:10,519
everything, everything seemed to run at me. This was very disagreeable to a guilty mind. The gates

11
00:01:10,519 --> 00:01:15,880
and dikes and banks came bursting at me through the mist, as if they cried as plainly as could be,

12
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:22,319
“A boy with somebody else’s pork pie! Stop him!” The cattle came upon me with like suddenness,

13
00:01:22,319 --> 00:01:29,759
staring out of their eyes, and steaming out of their nostrils, “Halloa, young thief!” One

14
00:01:29,759 --> 00:01:34,519
black ox, with a white cravat on,—who even had to my awakened conscience something of

15
00:01:34,519 --> 00:01:40,799
a clerical air,—fixed me so obstinately with his eyes, and moved his blunt head round in such an

16
00:01:40,799 --> 00:01:45,480
accusatory manner as I moved round, that I blubbered out to him, “I couldn’t help it,

17
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:52,000
sir! It wasn’t for myself I took it!” Upon which he put down his head, blew a cloud of smoke out

18
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:59,440
of his nose, and vanished with a kick-up of his hind-legs and a flourish of his tail.

19
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:06,079
All this time, I was getting on towards the river; but however fast I went, I couldn’t warm my feet,

20
00:02:06,079 --> 00:02:11,319
to which the damp cold seemed riveted, as the iron was riveted to the leg of the man I was

21
00:02:11,319 --> 00:02:16,960
running to meet. I knew my way to the Battery, pretty straight, for I had been down there on a

22
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:23,559
Sunday with Joe, and Joe, sitting on an old gun, had told me that when I was ’prentice to him,

23
00:02:23,559 --> 00:02:30,880
regularly bound, we would have such Larks there! However, in the confusion of the mist,

24
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:37,720
I found myself at last too far to the right, and consequently had to try back along the river-side,

25
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:44,720
on the bank of loose stones above the mud and the stakes that staked the tide out. Making my way

26
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:50,920
along here with all despatch, I had just crossed a ditch which I knew to be very near the Battery,

27
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:55,640
and had just scrambled up the mound beyond the ditch, when I saw the man sitting before

28
00:02:55,640 --> 00:03:04,320
me. His back was towards me, and he had his arms folded, and was nodding forward, heavy with sleep.

29
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:07,840
I thought he would be more glad if I came upon him with his breakfast,

30
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:12,519
in that unexpected manner, so I went forward softly and touched him on the

31
00:03:12,519 --> 00:03:19,119
shoulder. He instantly jumped up, and it was not the same man, but another man!

32
00:03:19,119 --> 00:03:24,440
And yet this man was dressed in coarse grey, too, and had a great iron on his leg,

33
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:29,519
and was lame, and hoarse, and cold, and was everything that the other man was;

34
00:03:29,519 --> 00:03:37,159
except that he had not the same face, and had a flat broad-brimmed low-crowned felt hat on.

35
00:03:37,159 --> 00:03:42,920
All this I saw in a moment, for I had only a moment to see it in: he swore an oath at me,

36
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:48,239
made a hit at me,—it was a round weak blow that missed me and almost knocked himself down,

37
00:03:48,239 --> 00:03:55,440
for it made him stumble,—and then he ran into the mist, stumbling twice as he went, and I lost him.

38
00:03:55,440 --> 00:04:02,000
“It’s the young man!” I thought, feeling my heart shoot as I identified him. I

39
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,119
dare say I should have felt a pain in my liver, too, if I had known where it was.

40
00:04:07,119 --> 00:04:11,639
I was soon at the Battery after that, and there was the right man,—hugging himself

41
00:04:11,639 --> 00:04:17,119
and limping to and fro, as if he had never all night left off hugging and limping,—waiting for

42
00:04:17,119 --> 00:04:22,880
me. He was awfully cold, to be sure. I half expected to see him drop down before my face

43
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:28,559
and die of deadly cold. His eyes looked so awfully hungry too, that when I handed him

44
00:04:28,559 --> 00:04:33,119
the file and he laid it down on the grass, it occurred to me he would have tried to eat it,

45
00:04:33,119 --> 00:04:39,559
if he had not seen my bundle. He did not turn me upside down this time to get at what I had,

46
00:04:39,559 --> 00:04:44,280
but left me right side upwards while I opened the bundle and emptied my pockets.

47
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:46,639
“What’s in the bottle, boy?” said he.

48
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:50,199
“Brandy,” said I.

49
00:04:50,199 --> 00:04:55,199
He was already handing mincemeat down his throat in the most curious manner,—more like a man who

50
00:04:55,199 --> 00:05:00,440
was putting it away somewhere in a violent hurry, than a man who was eating it,—but he

51
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:05,679
left off to take some of the liquor. He shivered all the while so violently, that it was quite

52
00:05:05,679 --> 00:05:11,280
as much as he could do to keep the neck of the bottle between his teeth, without biting it off.

53
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:14,719
“I think you have got the ague,” said I.

54
00:05:14,719 --> 00:05:18,400
“I’m much of your opinion, boy,” said he.

55
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:22,400
“It’s bad about here,” I told him. “You’ve been lying out on the meshes,

56
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:28,800
and they’re dreadful aguish. Rheumatic too.” “I’ll eat my breakfast afore they’re the death

57
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:34,400
of me,” said he. “I’d do that, if I was going to be strung up to that there gallows as there

58
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:40,039
is over there, directly afterwards. I’ll beat the shivers so far, I’ll bet you.”

59
00:05:40,039 --> 00:05:44,840
He was gobbling mincemeat, meatbone, bread, cheese, and pork pie, all at once:

60
00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:50,119
staring distrustfully while he did so at the mist all round us, and often stopping—even

61
00:05:50,119 --> 00:05:56,320
stopping his jaws—to listen. Some real or fancied sound, some clink upon the river

62
00:05:56,320 --> 00:06:01,960
or breathing of beast upon the marsh, now gave him a start, and he said, suddenly,—

63
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:05,639
“You’re not a deceiving imp? You brought no one with you?”

64
00:06:05,639 --> 00:06:07,880
“No, sir! No!”

65
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:10,679
“Nor giv’ no one the office to follow you?”

66
00:06:10,679 --> 00:06:12,320
“No!”

67
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:17,840
“Well,” said he, “I believe you. You’d be but a fierce young hound indeed,

68
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:21,039
if at your time of life you could help to hunt a wretched warmint

69
00:06:21,039 --> 00:06:25,519
hunted as near death and dunghill as this poor wretched warmint is!”

70
00:06:25,519 --> 00:06:29,000
Something clicked in his throat as if he had works in him like a clock,

71
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:35,320
and was going to strike. And he smeared his ragged rough sleeve over his eyes.

72
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:40,199
Pitying his desolation, and watching him as he gradually settled down upon the pie,

73
00:06:40,199 --> 00:06:44,079
I made bold to say, “I am glad you enjoy it.”

74
00:06:44,079 --> 00:06:45,400
“Did you speak?”

75
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:47,360
“I said I was glad you enjoyed it.”

76
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:50,719
“Thankee, my boy. I do.”

77
00:06:50,719 --> 00:06:56,800
I had often watched a large dog of ours eating his food; and I now noticed a decided similarity

78
00:06:56,800 --> 00:07:03,119
between the dog’s way of eating, and the man’s. The man took strong sharp sudden bites,

79
00:07:03,119 --> 00:07:10,800
just like the dog. He swallowed, or rather snapped up, every mouthful, too soon and too fast; and he

80
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:15,440
looked sideways here and there while he ate, as if he thought there was danger in every direction of

81
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:21,440
somebody’s coming to take the pie away. He was altogether too unsettled in his mind over it,

82
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:26,360
to appreciate it comfortably I thought, or to have anybody to dine with him, without making a

83
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:32,800
chop with his jaws at the visitor. In all of which particulars he was very like the dog.

84
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:37,360
“I am afraid you won’t leave any of it for him,” said I, timidly; after a silence during

85
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:42,679
which I had hesitated as to the politeness of making the remark. “There’s no more to be got

86
00:07:42,679 --> 00:07:49,000
where that came from.” It was the certainty of this fact that impelled me to offer the hint.

87
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:54,519
“Leave any for him? Who’s him?” said my friend, stopping in his crunching of pie-crust.

88
00:07:54,519 --> 00:07:58,400
“The young man. That you spoke of. That was hid with you.”

89
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:06,840
“Oh ah!” he returned, with something like a gruff laugh. “Him? Yes, yes! He don’t want no wittles.”

90
00:08:06,840 --> 00:08:09,159
“I thought he looked as if he did,” said I.

91
00:08:09,159 --> 00:08:14,960
The man stopped eating, and regarded me with the keenest scrutiny and the greatest surprise.

92
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:16,360
“Looked? When?”

93
00:08:16,880 --> 00:08:18,079
“Just now.”

94
00:08:18,079 --> 00:08:19,000
“Where?”

95
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:25,480
“Yonder,” said I, pointing; “over there, where I found him nodding asleep, and thought it was you.”

96
00:08:25,480 --> 00:08:28,039
He held me by the collar and stared at me so,

97
00:08:28,039 --> 00:08:33,239
that I began to think his first idea about cutting my throat had revived.

98
00:08:33,239 --> 00:08:39,000
“Dressed like you, you know, only with a hat,” I explained, trembling;

99
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:46,440
“and—and”—I was very anxious to put this delicately—“and with—the

100
00:08:46,440 --> 00:08:52,159
same reason for wanting to borrow a file. Didn’t you hear the cannon last night?”

101
00:08:52,159 --> 00:08:55,800
“Then there was firing!” he said to himself.

102
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:58,559
“I wonder you shouldn’t have been sure of that,” I returned,

103
00:08:58,559 --> 00:09:04,519
“for we heard it up at home, and that’s farther away, and we were shut in besides.”

104
00:09:04,519 --> 00:09:10,679
“Why, see now!” said he. “When a man’s alone on these flats, with a light head and a light

105
00:09:10,679 --> 00:09:16,360
stomach, perishing of cold and want, he hears nothin’ all night, but guns firing,

106
00:09:16,360 --> 00:09:22,559
and voices calling. Hears? He sees the soldiers, with their red coats lighted up by the torches

107
00:09:22,559 --> 00:09:29,039
carried afore, closing in round him. Hears his number called, hears himself challenged,

108
00:09:29,039 --> 00:09:34,639
hears the rattle of the muskets, hears the orders ‘Make ready! Present! Cover him steady,

109
00:09:34,639 --> 00:09:41,360
men!’ and is laid hands on—and there’s nothin’! Why, if I see one pursuing party

110
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:46,239
last night—coming up in order, Damn ’em, with their tramp, tramp—I see a hundred.

111
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:54,639
And as to firing! Why, I see the mist shake with the cannon, arter it was broad day,—But this man”;

112
00:09:54,639 --> 00:10:00,199
he had said all the rest, as if he had forgotten my being there; “did you notice anything in him?”

113
00:10:00,199 --> 00:10:05,280
“He had a badly bruised face,” said I, recalling what I hardly knew I knew.

114
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:10,599
“Not here?” exclaimed the man, striking his left cheek mercilessly, with the flat of his hand.

115
00:10:10,599 --> 00:10:11,800
“Yes, there!”

116
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:16,239
“Where is he?” He crammed what little food was left, into the breast of his grey jacket.

117
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:20,880
“Show me the way he went. I’ll pull him down, like a bloodhound. Curse this iron on

118
00:10:20,880 --> 00:10:26,599
my sore leg! Give us hold of the file, boy.” I indicated in what direction the mist had

119
00:10:26,599 --> 00:10:33,159
shrouded the other man, and he looked up at it for an instant. But he was down on the rank wet grass,

120
00:10:33,159 --> 00:10:39,800
filing at his iron like a madman, and not minding me or minding his own leg, which had an old chafe

121
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:44,760
upon it and was bloody, but which he handled as roughly as if it had no more feeling in it than

122
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:51,719
the file. I was very much afraid of him again, now that he had worked himself into this fierce hurry,

123
00:10:51,719 --> 00:10:58,000
and I was likewise very much afraid of keeping away from home any longer. I told him I must go,

124
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:05,559
but he took no notice, so I thought the best thing I could do was to slip off. The last I saw of him,

125
00:11:05,559 --> 00:11:11,800
his head was bent over his knee and he was working hard at his fetter, muttering impatient

126
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:18,519
imprecations at it and at his leg. The last I heard of him, I stopped in the mist to listen,

127
00:11:18,519 --> 00:11:21,000
and the file was still going. Chapter 4.
